# IViewportRenderer

`include/Umbra/IViewportRenderer.h`: the contract a host implements so that a `<viewport renderer="Name">` element can
show its scene, with no backend or runtime type in it.

```cpp
struct ViewportFrame { const std::uint8_t* Pixels; std::uint32_t Width, Height; }; // RGBA8, top row first
class IViewportRenderer {
    virtual std::optional<ViewportFrame> Render(std::uint32_t WidthPixels, std::uint32_t HeightPixels) = 0;
    virtual void PointerMoved(float X, float Y) {}                 // viewport pixels
    virtual void PointerButton(ViewportPointerButton Button, bool Down, const ViewportModifiers&) {}
    virtual void Wheel(float Notches, float NotchesX) {}
    virtual bool Key(std::string_view KeyName, bool Down, const ViewportModifiers&) { return false; }
    virtual void Tick(float Seconds) {}
    virtual void HoverChanged(bool PointerIsOver) {}
};
```

A backend uploads the pixels into whatever its renderer draws with; the host never touches the UI toolkit.

**Done when** penumbra-ui-backend builds `<viewport>` on it and Lantern's editor registers two renderers (the level
view and the asset preview) with no Penumbra code of its own.
